\frac{\dfrac{1}{2}\displaystyle\int_0^{2\pi}\mathrm{d}\theta\int_0^1 (1-r^4)r\,\mathrm{d}r}{\displaystyle\int_0^{2\pi}\mathrm{d}\theta\int_0^1 (1-r^2)r\,\mathrm{d}r}
=\frac{\dfrac{\pi}{3}}{\dfrac{\pi}{2}}=\frac{2}{3}.
$$

